Establish the shared frontend boundary needed for incremental migration while preserving working ModelSelection and SessionStats behavior. Canonical data remains owned by the backend, never TuiFrontEnd; projection objects remain frontend-owned and refreshed only on the tui-thread. Define neutral typed state declarations and registration/read interfaces so src/ava/tui consumers do not include Session or other backend implementation headers merely to access canonical data. Keep dependency direction acyclic and do not introduce a second registry or giant generic state bag when the existing FrontEnd/Data/UnlockedData mechanism suffices.

Specify and implement initialization, stable registration addresses, session replacement, notification lifetime, shutdown/unregistration and frontend thread confinement. Backend notification threads may set atomic dirty flags, but must not format strings, mutate local views or invoke terminal operations. Retain dirty_.exchange(false), independent per-output pending flags and full snapshots needed for future projections. For dependent projections such as context percentage, document who consumes each changed flag; one consumer must not silently steal another consumer's notification. Define coherent identity/revision capture for state spread across multiple canonical objects and reject stale asynchronous publication. Keep expensive computation outside locks and perform validated publication under bounded locks.

Define an app::frontend action boundary for commands, cancellation and decisions without Session references or backend capability aggregates in TUI. Action outcomes convey errors/control, not replacement state snapshots. Test initial unknown/known values, identical writes, changed-flag consumption order, updates during refresh, replacement, teardown and stale generations with deterministic synchronization. Existing model/stats and dependency tests must continue passing before migrating new domains.
